  472: export default router;
    1: /* crd.3b — the real room behind crd.2's door: the defender's own pool-builder
    2:  * for a contested roll, replacing the honest "coming soon" placeholder this
    3:  * file used to be.
    4:  *
    5:  * Entry contract, extended additively from crd.2's own (mirrors goTab(t) ->
    6:  * goTab(t, ctx)'s own precedent rather than inventing a new calling shape):
    7:  *
    8:  *     goTab('contested-resolve', { challengeId })
    9:  *       -> initContestedResolve(rootEl, ctx, chars)
   10:  *
   11:  * `chars` is the viewer's own full character list (crd.2's own
   12:  * `suiteState.chars`, already resident in memory — no new GET is added here;
   13:  * `app.js` already threads it into `initPendingQueue`, just not into this
   14:  * module until now).
   15:  *
   16:  * Trust boundary (crd.3a, unchanged and unmodified by this story): every
   17:  * aspect/Willpower/merit change calls the server's own PUT .../resolve and
   18:  * displays exactly the `defender_pool` it returns. Nothing here recomputes or
   19:  * guesses that number. The final commit calls the pre-existing, unmodified
   20:  * PUT .../accept — the actual dice roll ALREADY happens server-side there
   21:  * (crd.1), so this module never rolls its own dice. `mkColsEl` is imported
   22:  * from roll-v2.js purely to RENDER the rolls the server already decided
   23:  * (confirmed independent of that file's own pool-building `state` by reading
   24:  * it directly; it builds each die internally, so no separate die-builder
   25:  * import is needed here) — this module must never import `../shared/dice.js`'s
   26:  * `rollPool`/`d10`, which would silently perform a SECOND, illegitimate
   27:  * client-side roll alongside the server's real one.
   28:  */
