    1: /**
    2:  * crd.3b — the real client resolution screen (Epic CRD).
    3:  *
    4:  * Coverage map:
    5:  *   AC1  — initContestedResolve(rootEl, ctx, chars) additive signature
    6:  *   AC2  — missing challenge/character renders a graceful, non-crashing state
    7:  *   AC3  — aspect segmented control shows real effective attribute values
    8:  *   AC4  — Willpower toggle labelled +2, disabled at zero live WP
    9:  *   AC5  — merit chips: narrow 2-merit set only, empty state otherwise
   10:  *   AC6  — every selection change calls PUT .../resolve; a stale response
   11:  *          arriving after a newer one must not overwrite it (generation guard)
   12:  *   AC7  — commit calls PUT .../accept (unmodified) and renders the outcome
   13:  *          via roll-v2.js's own mkColsEl, never re-rolling anything itself
   14:  *   AC9  — "Defending as" banner only for a multi-character player
   15:  *
   16:  * TESTING APPROACH — following crd.2's own precedent exactly
   17:  * (crd-2-pending-queue.test.js's own stated rationale): vi.mock() the
   18:  * browser-only imports and drive the real module against a hand-rolled
   19:  * element stub, the one real behavioural precedent this repo has for driving
   20:  * a browser module in Node (dt-form-territory-fresh-fetch.test.js). No jsdom
   21:  * — adding one is a HALT condition.
   22:  *
   23:  * `tracker.js` and `roll-v2.js` are mocked outright rather than loaded for
   24:  * real: this story's own use of `ensureLoaded`/`trackerRead` is a UX
   25:  * convenience only (the server re-checks live Willpower independently on
   26:  * every crd.3a `/resolve` call regardless of what this screen shows), and
   27:  * `mkColsEl` is a pure DOM-builder this story only needs to prove it CALLS
   28:  * correctly, not re-verify. Neither module's own correctness is this story's
   29:  * concern (gdx-7's own test already proves tracker.js's real read/write
   30:  * behaviour against a live DB).
   31:  */
